ECS EC2 vs Fargate -  Legacy, Tech, and when to use what

Amazon ECS offers two powerful launch types: The classic EC2 and serverless Fargate. Both are robust, but they cater to different needs.

🔧 1. EC2 Launch Type - Full Control, Classic but Powerful Approach

Host-level management: You provision and manage EC2 instances, Create ECS Clusters, Services, Task definitions, integrate your services with Load balancer in turn with Auto scaling groups and manage AMIs and security.

Hardware/customization: Supports GPUs, custom OS kernel modules, custom networking, persistence via EBS, and daemon containers.

Resource optimization: Use Spot/Reserved Instances, and tightly bin-pack workloads to reduce costs at scale.

Debugging: ECS Exec allows shell into containers, even on EC2, via SSM.

Use it when:
✔️ You need absolute infra control
✔️ Running stable, long-lived, density-optimized workloads
✔️ Using GPUs or system-level dependencies
✔️ Cutting costs via Spot/Reserved Instances

☁️ 2. Fargate Launch Type - Serverless Simplicity

True serverless: No EC2 to manage. AWS handles provisioning and scaling. No need of Load balancers/Auto scaling groups. Just define vCPU & memory and required stuff in your Task Definition.

Fast start: Tasks spin up in ~30-60 seconds. SOCI image indexing further speeds things.

Isolation & security: Each task runs in its own ENI with IAM task roles and strong network isolation.

Limits: Max 16 vCPU / 120 GiB RAM, one ENI per task - plan your subnet IPs since each task consumes a Private IP, which can exhaust Subnet IPs.

Use it when:
 ✔️ You want zero infra ops
 ✔️ Deploying microservices, batch jobs, event-driven tasks
 ✔️ Handling bursty or unpredictable workloads
 ✔️ Seeking fast deployment without managing hosts

Fargate reduces operational workload but it is dangerous when you see

1. Unexpected high costs.
2. Realize limited customization.
3. Long running tasks at scale.
4. Cold start delays
5. Networking(subnet IPs) costs and limits.
